{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And you shall take for yourselves.</b> The Sages said: of your own — to exclude the borrowed and the stolen, so that his advocate not become his accuser. A parable: a king who sent his agent to collect his levy in a city. After he had collected, a certain townsman found him, robbed him, and took all his vessels and everything in his hand. Some days later, that townsman needed to be judged before the king. He came to hire someone to plead merit for him before the king. That agent found him. He said to him: What are you doing here? He said to him: I have a case before the king, and I seek to hire someone to speak on my behalf. He said to him: Give me what you robbed from me and I will speak on your behalf. He returned the vessels to him alone and went his way. The next day he came to be judged before the king. He asked: Do you have a man to plead merit for you? He said to him: So-and-so is here, who will plead merit for me. He said to him: What merit do you have to plead on this one's behalf? He said to them: I will tell you what this friend of mine did to me — when I went to collect the king's levy from such-and-such a city, he ambushed me on the way and robbed me and took all that I had, and these vessels are some of the vessels that he returned to me on condition that I plead merit for him. All began and said: Woe to this one, whose advocate has become his accuser. (And in the Midrash it is stated: his advocate — his accuser.)" "<b>On the first day.</b> The lulav that we take on the first day is a sign that we were vindicated in judgment on the Day of Atonement, as it is said: ״אז ירננו עצי היער מלפני ה׳ כי בא לשפוט את הארץ״. It speaks of Israel and the nations of the world, whom the Holy One, blessed be He, judges on the Day of Atonement — these and those enter into judgment, and we do not know who is victorious. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: Take your lulavs in your hands, and all will know that you were vindicated in judgment. A parable: two men who entered to be judged before the king, and the people did not know who won — only the one who comes out with a white staff or an apple in his hand, then they know that he emerged vindicated from before the king." "<b>Fruit of a beautiful tree.</b> These four species — among them there is one that has taste and fragrance, like the etrog, and it corresponds to the righteous, who have the fragrance of Torah and the taste of good deeds. In the tree on which the lulav grows, there is taste but no fragrance, and it corresponds to the intermediate ones of Israel, who have the taste of commandments but do not have the fragrance of Torah. The myrtle has fragrance but no taste, and it corresponds to those who have the fragrance of Torah but do not have commandments. And the willow has neither taste nor fragrance, and it corresponds to the ignorant, who have neither the fragrance of Torah nor the taste of commandments. And we bind the four together — a hint that the Holy One, blessed be He, is not reconciled to Israel until they are all one band, as it is said: ״הבונה בשמיו מעלותיו ואגודתו על ארץ יסדה״. When is the Holy One, blessed be He, exalted? When we all become one band. And it appears to us that this is why we say on Rosh HaShanah: ״ויעשו כלם אגודה אחת״. Another interpretation: the lulav resembles the spine, the myrtle resembles the eye, the willow resembles the lips, the etrog resembles the heart. And you have no limb greater than these, and they are equivalent to the entire body. And this is what David said: ״כל עצמותי תאמרנה ה׳ מי כמוך״ — that there is to praise the Holy One, blessed be He, with the mouth and the heart and the eye and the spine, which is the entire body, for it depends on the spine."], :book :leviticus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:08:36.799692035Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}